@using MUI.Catalog.Persistence
@using MUI.Web.Localization
@using MUI.Discovery
@using MUI.Web.Accounts

@*
    What a claim actually grants, as two forms (spec §8.5, §11).

    Everything here is enrichment: the fields MSSP has no room for, and the owner's decision about
    their own connect screen. Nothing on this panel can reach a player count, a capability or a
    reachability history — the writable set is the field registry's own OwnerEnrichable flag, read
    below rather than listed here, and the endpoint refuses anything outside it out loud.

    An owner's answer is DECLARED. It is stored under its own field source beside whatever the
    crawler measured, shown with its age like every other fact, and it never replaces, hides or
    silences a measurement. The panel says so, because an operator typing into a box on a site whose
    whole claim is that its data is measured deserves to know which of the two they are producing.
*@

<section class="owner-panel">
    <h3>@Say("owner.declare.heading", ("game", Name))</h3>

    @*
        The provenance word is placed rather than written into the sentence, and it is the SAME id
        the chip on the game's page uses. That is deliberate: this paragraph promises an owner what
        their page will say, and two ids for one word is how the promise and the page drift apart.
    *@
    <p class="faint">
        @foreach (var part in Sentence.Place(Tag, "owner.declare.lede", ["declared"]))
        {
            @if (part.Slot is "declared")
            {
                <em>@L("provenance.game.ownerDeclared")</em>
            }
            else
            {
                @part.Text
            }
        }
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/enrichment")" class="enrichment">
        <AntiforgeryToken />

        @foreach (var definition in FieldRegistry.OwnerEnrichable)
        {
            <label>
                @* The registry's own field name: machine voice, like a hostname or a codebase. *@
                <span class="kicker" translate="no">@definition.Name.ToLowerInvariant()</span>
                <input type="text"
                       name="@(OwnerWrites.FieldPrefix + definition.Name)"
                       value="@(Current(definition.Name)?.Value)"
                       maxlength="@OwnerEnrichment.MaxValueLength" />
                @if (Current(definition.Name) is { Value.Length: > 0 } row)
                {
                    @* "declared", never "measured" — this is the age of what the owner told us. *@
                    <span class="faint">
                        @Say("owner.field.declared", ("age", Relative.Ago(Tag, Now - row.LastConfirmedAt)))
                    </span>
                }
            </label>
        }

        <button type="submit">@L("owner.save")</button>
    </form>

    @*
        The overridable half (§8.5). MSSP has a variable for every one of these, so the form shows
        what the game reports beside the box rather than offering an empty one — and says plainly
        that fixing mush.cnf is the better repair, because an answer typed here reaches this site and
        no other crawler.

        The report is shown as text and never as the box's value. Pre-filling an editable field with
        a game's own report invites somebody to retype it into an override saying exactly the same
        thing: a second row, a second age, a second thing to keep current, in exchange for nothing.
    *@
    <h3>@Say("owner.override.heading", ("game", Name))</h3>

    @*
        Three sentences rather than one with a <strong> inside it. An emphasised clause in the
        middle of a sentence is one a translator cannot move — it lands elsewhere in most languages
        and nowhere in some — and this is the clause that must survive translation intact, because
        it is the site's whole claim. Its own id, its own sentence, its own element.
    *@
    @*
        The placed sentence sits in its own <span>, and that is load-bearing rather than tidy:
        Razor drops the whitespace on a line that opens a code block, so a bare @foreach after a
        sibling sentence renders as "…not an hour of reachability.If a line below…". Between two
        elements the space is ordinary markup and survives.
    *@
    <p class="faint">
        @L("owner.override.lede")
        <strong>@L("owner.override.nothingMeasured")</strong>
        <span>
            @foreach (var part in Sentence.Place(Tag, "owner.override.fixItThere", ["file"]))
            {
                @if (part.Slot is "file")
                {
                    <code translate="no">mush.cnf</code>
                }
                else
                {
                    @part.Text
                }
            }
        </span>
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/enrichment")" class="enrichment override">
        <AntiforgeryToken />

        @foreach (var definition in FieldRegistry.OwnerOverridable)
        {
            <label>
                <span class="kicker" translate="no">@definition.Name.ToLowerInvariant()</span>
                <input type="text"
                       name="@(OwnerWrites.FieldPrefix + definition.Name)"
                       value="@(Current(definition.Name)?.Value)"
                       maxlength="@OwnerEnrichment.MaxValueLength" />
                @if (Reported.TryGetValue(definition.Name, out var reported) && reported.Value.Length > 0)
                {
                    @*
                        What the game itself reports, shown beside the box and never in it. The
                        value is the game's own bytes and is placed as markup rather than
                        translated.
                    *@
                    <span class="faint">
                        @foreach (var part in Sentence.Place(
                            Tag,
                            "owner.report.value",
                            ["value"],
                            ("age", Relative.Ago(Tag, Now - reported.LastConfirmedAt))))
                        {
                            @if (part.Slot is "value")
                            {
                                <span class="mono" translate="no">@reported.Value</span>
                            }
                            else
                            {
                                @part.Text
                            }
                        }
                    </span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span class="faint">@L("owner.report.none")</span>
                }
            </label>
        }

        <button type="submit">@L("owner.save")</button>
    </form>

    @*
        The one field on this form that moves more than a row. Said here rather than in a tooltip,
        because a URL changing is the kind of surprise somebody should have read about first.
    *@
    <p class="faint">@Say("owner.rename.note", ("game", Name))</p>

    <h3>@L("owner.screen.heading")</h3>

    @if (Suppressed)
    {
        <p>@L("owner.screen.suppressed")</p>
        <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/connect-screen")">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="suppress" value="false" />
            <button type="submit">@L("owner.screen.show")</button>
        </form>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>@L("owner.screen.shown")</p>
        <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/connect-screen")">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="suppress" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">@L("owner.screen.stop")</button>
        </form>
    }

    @*
        §11's third route. The other two — the MSSP variable and the DNS TXT record — are answered by
        the game itself and need no account, which is why they are named here beside the button
        rather than hidden behind it: an owner who would rather not depend on us having their claim
        on file can say it in their own config and we will honour it on the next cycle.
    *@
    <h3>@L("owner.crawl.heading")</h3>

    @if (OptOut is { AllStopped: true })
    {
        @*
            The empty hours this leaves may not be given a cause (rule 2, rule 5): "you asked us to
            stop" is OUR fact and not a measurement of the game. The clause is emphasised and is
            placed by the message, so a language that puts it first can.
        *@
        <p>
            @foreach (var part in Sentence.Place(Tag, "owner.crawl.stopped", ["ourFact"], ("game", Name)))
            {
                @if (part.Slot is "ourFact")
                {
                    <em>@L("owner.crawl.ourFact")</em>
                }
                else
                {
                    @part.Text
                }
            }
        </p>

        @if (OptOut.WithdrawableHere)
        {
            <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/crawl")">
                <AntiforgeryToken />
                <input type="hidden" name="stop" value="false" />
                <button type="submit">@L("owner.crawl.resume")</button>
            </form>
        }
        else
        {
            @* The game is still saying stop on every probe, and this side may not overrule it. *@
            <p class="faint">@Say("owner.crawl.standing", ("routes", Routes))</p>
        }
    }
    else if (OptOut is { Partial: true })
    {
        @*
            Neither state, and shown as neither. Rounding this to "stopped" would tell an owner we
            had left them alone while we went on dialling the port that is still open.
        *@
        <p class="warn">
            @Say(
                "owner.crawl.partial",
                ("stopped", string.Join(", ", OptOut.Stopped)),
                ("dialling", string.Join(", ", OptOut.Dialling)))
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/crawl")">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="stop" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">@L("owner.crawl.stopAll")</button>
        </form>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>@Say("owner.crawl.dialling", ("game", Name))</p>
        @*
            An opt-out is honoured and is never a deletion — the first clause says so, and the two
            config spellings are the crawler's own tokens rather than words to translate.
        *@
        <p class="faint">
            @foreach (var part in Sentence.Place(Tag, "owner.crawl.selfService", ["mssp", "dns"]))
            {
                @if (part.Slot is "mssp")
                {
                    <code translate="no">@OptOutVocabulary.MsspVariable</code>
                }
                else if (part.Slot is "dns")
                {
                    <code translate="no">@OptOutVocabulary.DnsLabel</code>
                }
                else
                {
                    @part.Text
                }
            }
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/crawl")">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="stop" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">@L("owner.crawl.stop")</button>
        </form>
    }

    @*
        The second decision (migration 0025), and it is a second one rather than a stronger version of
        the first. Stopping the crawl is what most people who press that button want; this is for the
        ones who want the page out of the directory as well, and it is offered only underneath a
        standing opt-out on every address — a game we are still dialling has not made the first
        decision, and there is nothing here to extend.
    *@
    @if (Listing is { IsUnlisted: true })
    {
        <h3>@L("owner.listing.heading")</h3>

        @* Unlisted, never deleted (§7.5): the page and every URL it ever had go on answering. *@
        <p>@Say("owner.listing.unlisted", ("game", Name))</p>

        <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/listing")">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="unlist" value="false" />
            <button type="submit">@L("owner.listing.relist")</button>
        </form>

        <p class="faint">@L("owner.listing.probeRelists")</p>
    }
    else if (Listing is { MayUnlist: true })
    {
        <h3>@L("owner.listing.heading")</h3>

        <p>@L("owner.listing.mayUnlist")</p>

        <p class="faint">@L("owner.listing.reversible")</p>

        <form method="post" action="@Http.Link($"/account/games/{GameId}/listing")">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="unlist" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">@L("owner.listing.unlist")</button>
        </form>
    }
</section>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private HttpContext? Http { get; set; }

    private string Tag => Http.LocaleOf().Tag;

    private string L(string id) => Messages.For(Tag, id);

    private string Say(string id, params (string Key, object? Value)[] args) =>
        Messages.Say(Tag, id, args);

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Guid GameId { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// What this game's owners have already declared, keyed by field.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Owner rows only, and passed in rather than fetched here so that one page load reads the
    /// catalogue once. A measurement is deliberately not offered back as a draft of itself: an
    /// editable box pre-filled with something we measured is an invitation to overwrite it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, GameField> Declared { get; set; } =
        new Dictionary<string, GameField>(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>
    /// What the game reports about itself, for the boxes that override a report.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Read beside <see cref="Declared"/> in the same pass, and shown as text rather than used as a
    /// value — see <c>OwnerEnrichment.ReportedAsync</c> for why an editable box is never pre-filled
    /// with somebody's own MSSP.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, GameField> Reported { get; set; } =
        new Dictionary<string, GameField>(StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    [Parameter] public DateTimeOffset Now { get; set; } = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which of this game's addresses we are honouring an opt-out for, or null where there is no
    /// register to ask — the demo fixture, which dials nothing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public OwnerOptOutState? OptOut { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this game is in the listing and whether its owner may take it out (migration 0025), or
    /// null where nothing here records that — the demo fixture, which has no register to ask.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Null and "may not" render identically, and deliberately: a control offered where we cannot
    /// tell whether we are still crawling somebody would be the panel guessing on their behalf.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public OwnerListingState? Listing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The routes the standing rules came in by, for the case this side cannot undo.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// One id per route rather than an English clause built round a constant. The variable and the
    /// DNS label are the crawler's own tokens and go in as arguments: a translated
    /// <c>MUINDEX OPT-OUT</c> is one no probe would ever read.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Routes => string.Join(", ", (OptOut?.Standing ?? [])
        .Select(rule => rule.Source switch
        {
            OptOutSource.Mssp =>
                Say("owner.crawl.route.mssp", ("variable", OptOutVocabulary.MsspVariable)),
            OptOutSource.DnsTxt =>
                Say("owner.crawl.route.dns", ("label", OptOutVocabulary.DnsLabel)),
            _ => L("owner.crawl.route.recorded"),
        })
        .Distinct());

    /// <summary>
    /// The owner's own decision, read from the field they set rather than from the rendered page.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The surfaces resolve suppression through the §5.1 precedence ladder, which is right for a
    /// reader and wrong for this form: the control has to reflect what <em>this owner</em> asked for
    /// and offer the opposite of it, and a ladder that ever put another source above theirs would
    /// otherwise show them a button that does nothing.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool Suppressed => string.Equals(
        Current(InternalFields.ConnectScreenSuppressed)?.Value, "true", StringComparison.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>
    /// The owner's row for a field, withdrawn or not.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A withdrawn field keeps an empty row (nothing is ever deleted), and the box beside it must
    /// not then claim it was "declared 5w ago" — an age on a value nobody can see is an age on
    /// nothing. Callers that render a declaration check the value as well as the row.
    /// </remarks>
    private GameField? Current(string field) =>
        Declared.TryGetValue(field, out var row) ? row : null;
}
